@startuml vib-stream
' How samples become a line on the wire, and every point where the right
' answer is to say nothing. Grounded in Src/vib_telemetry.c
' (vib_telemetry_poll), Src/adxl_port.c (the stream adapter) and Src/main.c
' (service_accelerometer).

title One vib line, from the sensor's queue to the wire

skinparam shadowing false

start

:main loop calls poll(now_ms);

if (VIB_POLL_PERIOD_MS passed?) then (no)
  :return, nothing asked;
  note right
    The loop turns about twenty
    thousand times a second, measured
    on the board. Asking the sensor on
    every turn would put twenty thousand
    transactions a second on a bus that
    carries a hundred thousand bits.
  end note
  stop
else (yes)
endif

:ask how many samples are waiting;

if (the bus answered?) then (no)
  :throw the window away, send nothing;
  stop
else (yes)
endif

if (the queue is FULL?) then (yes)
  :throw the window away, send nothing;
  note right
    Full means the sensor had nowhere
    to put the next sample, so what is
    in there is not consecutive with
    what the window already holds.
  end note
  stop
else (no)
endif

repeat
  :take one sample;
  if (the bus answered?) then (no)
    :throw the window away, stop early;
    stop
  else (yes)
  endif
  :push it into the window;
  if (window complete, 160 samples?) then (yes)
    :send `vib,<milli-g>` + newline;
  else (no)
  endif
repeat while (more were waiting?) is (yes)

:return;

stop

legend bottom
  Every refusal above sends NOTHING, never a number. A root mean square
  over 200 ms with a hole in it is not a root mean square over 200 ms, and
  a value on this wire is one the host cannot tell apart from a reading.
  Whether the sensor is there is said separately, in words, every two
  seconds, by adxl_report.

  20 ms is half the time the sensor's 32 deep queue takes to overflow at
  800 Hz, so one missed turn of the loop costs nothing.
endlegend

@enduml
